#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125269} :activity/id #uuid "c9d087b0-e0c7-5257-8039-6b926add5c2f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781 :topic/id #uuid "be3a8d83-d961-5a49-82b6-e2c59f1bcd4b" :topic/math-academy-id 1933} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125724 :tutorial/content "Just like we can compute second derivatives of single-variable functions, we can also compute second derivatives of multivariable functions.\n\nFor example, consider the two-variable function\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nIts two first-order partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}+y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2xy+x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_x(x,y)$ and $f_y(x,y)$ are themselves functions that are differentiable with respect to both $x$ and $y,$ we can compute the **second-order partial derivatives** as well. \n\n- The partial derivatives of $f_x(x,y)$ with respect to $x$ and $y,$ denoted $f_{xx}$ and $f_{xy}$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y))={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y))=2y+xy{e}^{xy}+{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The partial derivatives of $f_y(x,y)$ with respect to $x$ and $y,$ denoted $f_{yx}$ and $f_{yy}$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y))=2y+xy{e}^{xy}+{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y))=2x+{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivatives $f_{xy}$ and $f_{yx}$ are called **mixed partial derivatives** because they involve taking the partial derivatives with respect to different variables, $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe following alternative notations are also widely used to represent second-order partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{x}^{2}}(f) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}={f}_{xx}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{y}^{2}}(f)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}={f}_{yy} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂y\\,∂x}(f) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y\\,∂x}={f}_{xy}\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x\\,∂y}(f)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x\\,∂y}={f}_{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In our example, the two mixed partial derivatives were equal:\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y) = 2y + xye^{xy} + e^{xy} = f_{yx}(x,y)\n$$\n\nLater, we will see that under rather mild conditions on $f,$ we almost always have that $f_{xy}=f_{yx}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "feb54aa0-17fb-5468-8b99-14174a6bd059" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2397 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0eaae658-c43c-5756-80c4-c4462162b555" :step/math-academy-id 10558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124440 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124244 :question/id #uuid "0a8f5141-941d-5059-9d9f-bf9e5b09096f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ea31275-77bd-59c7-8e1d-96e2e4b252a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382372}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Mixed Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "4f4f64aa-ab4f-5abb-8d9c-ca2aa2790b64" :step/math-academy-id 10559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124441 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124245 :question/id #uuid "e16f6686-dffb-554e-a50a-8760e818439f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3129"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a0bfe-7c69-5ad0-ad1d-aa8692dae673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382386}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Expressions Involving Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "a25341f7-8d06-561b-9802-42d2f9c792e8" :step/math-academy-id 10563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125725 :tutorial/content "We can also define other **higher order partial derivatives** such as $f_{xxy}, \\, f_{xxyy}, \\, f_{xyxy},$ etc. \n\nFor instance, the function $f(x,y,z)= z\\cos(xy)$ has the first-order partial derivative\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y,z) = -xz \\sin(xy)\n$$\n\nand the second-order partial derivative\n\n$$\nf_{yx}(x,y,z)= -x yz \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - z\\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate again, with respect to $x,$ then we get the **third-order partial derivative** $f_{yxx}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xyz\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-z\\operatorname{sin}(xy)) \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xyz(-\\operatorname{sin}(xy)\\cdot y)-z\\operatorname{cos}(xy)\\cdot y \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}(xy)-2yz\\operatorname{cos}(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to compute a $n$th-order partial derivative, we need to compute a sequence of $n$ individual derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "e256986d-ca95-50b3-a16c-0f46005b5c66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2398 :tutorial/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives for Functions of  More Than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f8b4cf47-e81c-516c-8eef-7d544743362a" :step/math-academy-id 10566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124246 :question/id #uuid "a41bfb06-4372-5124-b910-4b35f19d148b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83724b66-0f15-5b94-9576-76e9dfd56d38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382131}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Higher-Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "25a77626-7a96-5d1b-b087-a01012cbeb91" :step/math-academy-id 10567}] :activity/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124726}]}
